      "text": "To find the rightmost three digits of \\(5^{1993}\\), we need to compute \\(5^{1993} \\mod 1000\\). We will use the Chinese Remainder Theorem (CRT) and the method of successive squaring to achieve this.\n\nFirst, we need to compute \\(5^{1993} \\mod 8\\) and \\(5^{1993} \\mod 125\\), since \\(1000 = 8 \\times 125\\) and the remainders modulo 8 and 125 will be the same modulo 1000.\n\n### Step 1: Compute \\(5^{1993} \\mod 8\\)\nAny power of 5 is congruent to \\(5 \\equiv 5 \\mod 8\\), so:\n\\[5^{1993} \\equiv 5 \\text{ mod 8}.\\]\n\n### Step 2: Compute \\(5^{1993} \\mod 125\\)\nWe can use Euler's theorem, which states that for any integer \\(a\\) and \\(n\\) such that \\(\\gcd(a, n) = 1\\), \\(a^{\\phi(n)} \\equiv 1 \\mod n\\), where \\(\\phi\\) is the Euler's totient function. For \\(n = 125\\), \\(\\phi(125) = 125 \\left(1 - \\frac{1}{5}\\right) = 100\\), so:\n\\[5^{100} \\equiv 1 \\mod 125.\\]\nSince 1993 is congruent to 93 modulo 100, we have:\n\\[5^{1993} \\equiv 5^{93} \\mod 125.\\]\nNow, we need to compute \\(5^{93} \\mod 125\\). Notice that:\n\\[5^3 = 125 \\equiv 0 \\mod 125.\\]\nTherefore, any power of 5 greater than or equal to 3 will also be congruent to 0 modulo 125:\n\\[5^{93} \\equiv 0 \\mod 125.\\]\nThus, \\(5^{1993} \\equiv 0 \\mod 125\\).\n\n### Step 3: Combine the results using the Chinese Remainder Theorem\nWe have:\n\\[5^{1993} \\equiv 5 \\mod 8,\\]\n\\[5^{1993} \\equiv 0 \\mod 125.\\]\nWe need to find a number \\(x\\) such that:\n\\[x \\equiv 5 \\mod 8,\\]\n\\[x \\equiv 0 \\mod 125.\\]\nThis means \\(x = 125k\\) for some integer \\(k\\), and substituting into the first congruence, we get:\n\\[125k \\equiv 5 \\mod 8.\\]\nSince \\(125 \\equiv 5 \\mod 8\\), this simplifies to:\n\\[5k \\equiv 5 \\mod 8.\\]\nDividing both sides by 5 (since 5 and 8 are coprime), we get:\n\\[k \\equiv 1 \\mod 8.\\]\nSo, \\(k = 8m + 1\\) for some integer \\(m\\), and substituting back, we get:\n\\[x = 125k = 125(8m + 1) = 1000m + 125.\\]\nThus, \\(x \\equiv 125 \\mod 1000\\).\n\nThe rightmost three digits of \\(5^{1993}\\) are \\(\\boxed{125}\\).",
