      "text": "   \n   A. ${{5}^{1}}=5$\n   B. ${{5}^{2}}=25$\n   C. ${{5}^{3}}=125$\n   D. ${{5}^{4}}=625$\n   \nProof: We can find the pattern. The powers of 5 are:\n   \n   \\[\\begin{align} 5^1&=5 \\\\  \n  5^2&=25\\\\  \n  5^3&=125\\\\  \n  5^4&=625\\\\  \n  \\end{align}\\] The last three digits of $5^{1993}$ are the same as the last three digits of $5^3$, which are 125.\n   \nTherefore, the correct answer is \\boxed{C}.\n\nTo find the rightmost three digits of $5^{1993}$, we need to compute $5^{1993} \\mod 1000$. This can be done by first finding the pattern in the powers of 5 modulo 1000.\n\nUsing the solution provided, we can see that the powers of 5 modulo 1000 follow a certain pattern:\n\\[\n\\begin{align}\n5^1 & =5 \\\\\n5^2 & =25 \\\\\n5^3 & =125 \\\\\n5^4 & =625 \\\\\n\\end{align}\n\\]\nThis pattern repeats every three powers. Since $1993 = 3 \\cdot 664 + 1$, we can say that the last three digits of $5^{1993}$ are the same as the last three digits of $5^3$, which are 125.\n\nThus, the rightmost three digits of $5^{1993}$ are $\\boxed{125}$.",
