      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "We are given that $5 \\times 25 = 125$; $5 \\times 625 = 3125$; etc... Our answer is $\\boxed{125}$. \nAlternatively, we directly calculate $5^{1993} \\mod 1000$. We could use Fermat's Little Theorem to find the answer $\\boxed{125}$.\n\nLet's solve the problem step by step.\n\nThe rightmost three digits of a number \\( n \\) are the same as its value modulo 1000. So, we need to find \\( 5^{1993} \\mod 1000 \\).\n\nFermat's Little Theorem states that if \\( p \\) is a prime number and \\( a \\) is an integer such that \\( a \\) is not divisible by \\( p \\), then \\( a^{p-1} \\equiv 1 \\mod p \\).\n\nIn this problem, \\( p = 10 \\) (since \\( 1000 = 2^3 \\times 5^3 \\)) and \\( a = 5 \\). So, by Fermat's Little Theorem, \\( 5^{999} \\equiv 1 \\mod 10 \\). This means that we can break down \\( 5^{1993} \\) into \\( 5^{1992+1} \\equiv (5^{999})^{2} \\times 5 \\equiv 1 \\times 5 \\equiv 5 \\mod 10 \\).\n\nThis simplifies our problem to finding \\( 5^{1993} \\mod 8 \\).\n\nSince \\( 5 \\equiv 5 \\mod 8 \\) and \\( 5^3 \\equiv 5 \\mod 8 \\) (and so on), we can see that any power of 5 that is a multiple of 3 will be congruent to 5 modulo 8. Therefore, \\( 5^{1993} \\equiv 5 \\mod 8 \\).\n\nNow, we combine these results using the Chinese Remainder Theorem (CRT): \\( 5^{1993} \\mod 1000 \\). We need to find a number \\( x \\) such that:\n\n1. \\( x \\equiv 5 \\mod 10 \\)\n2. \\( x \\equiv 5 \\mod 8 \\)\n\nSince both congruences are satisfied by any number that is congruent to 5 modulo 10, we can conclude that \\( x \\equiv 5 \\mod 1000 \\). Therefore, the rightmost three digits of \\( 5^{1993} \\) are the same as the rightmost three digits of 5, which is 005.\n\nLet's verify this with Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\n# Calculate 5^1993 mod 1000\nresult = pow(5, 1993, 1000)\n\n# Extract the last three digits\nrightmost_three_digits = result % 1000\nprint(rightmost_three_digits)\n```",
